{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-6461",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The following triangle illustrates the information given.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14266767/assets/e9674b5a0921c9fb8d936c435351d0557e90984e4662e9a510a6889812bbad38.png)\n\nThe Law of Sines states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡A}=\\frac{b}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡B}=\\frac{c}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡C}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\nm∠B={47}^{∘},\\,m∠C={62}^{∘},\\,c=20,\n$$\n\nand consequently\n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{47}^{∘}}=\\frac{20}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{62}^{∘}}.\n$$\n\nTo solve for $b,$ we multiply both sides by $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{47}^{∘}$ to give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{47}^{∘}\\times \\frac{b}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{47}^{∘}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡{47}^{∘}\\times \\frac{20}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{62}^{∘}} \\\\ \\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{47}^{∘}}\\times \\frac{b}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{47}^{∘}}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡{47}^{∘}\\times \\frac{20}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{62}^{∘}} \\\\ b & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡{47}^{∘}\\times \\frac{20}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{62}^{∘}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{47}^{∘}=0.731\\,35$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{62}^{∘}=0.882\\,95,$ both rounded to five decimal places to avoid rounding error. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡{47}^{∘}\\times \\frac{20}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{62}^{∘}} \\\\  & ≈0.731\\,35\\times \\frac{20}{0.882\\,95} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{0.731\\,35\\times 20}{0.882\\,95} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{14.627}{0.882\\,95} \\\\  & ≈16.57,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places.",
